! v0.72.0 test: HOOH torsion barrier transition state.
!
! H2O2 (hydrogen peroxide) has two equivalent skew minima around
! a torsion angle of +/- 113 deg, with two distinct rotational
! barriers between them:
!   * trans barrier: TS at HOOH dihedral = 180 deg  (lower; ~ 1 kcal/mol
!     at HF/STO-3G; experimentally ~1.1 kcal/mol)
!   * cis barrier:   TS at HOOH dihedral =   0 deg  (higher; ~ 8-10 kcal/mol
!     at HF/STO-3G; experimentally ~ 7 kcal/mol)
!
! This input targets the TRANS barrier (HOOH = 180 deg).  At the
! TS the four atoms are coplanar.  Imaginary mode is the torsion
! rotation around the O-O axis (~ 250-350i cm^-1 at HF/STO-3G).
!
! Initial guess: planar trans geometry (HOOH dihedral exactly 180);
! we sit ON the saddle by symmetry, so P-RFO has very little work
! to do but the test exercises the full pipeline.  The
! displacement-criterion alternate exit catches this case quickly.
%method  HF
%basis   sto-3g
%job     ts+freq
%charge  0
%mult    1
%hess_method  analytical
%ts_mode 1
%geom_maxiter 60
%geom angstrom
O   0.000   0.000   0.737
O   0.000   0.000  -0.737
H   0.945   0.000   1.012
H  -0.945   0.000  -1.012
*end
